=> radius 8. Z -18.6 to 4 => height 22.6.\n\nBore diameter 8.2 => radius 4.1. Z -19.6 to 8.8 => height 28.4.\n\nRim inner diameter 49.8 => radius 24.9. outer diameter 64 => radius 32. height 4.1. bottom Z=42.4.\n\nAll good.\n\nPotential issue: The outer cone frustum top diameter 56.8 > rim outer diameter 64? Actually 56.8 <64, so rim extends beyond outer top. That's fine.\n\nNow produce final output.\n\nOne more check: makeCone direction vector length? It probably normalizes? Might be okay.\n\nSome CadQuery versions require direction as Vector, yes.\n\nReturn exactly one Python code block.\n\nLet's output.<|eom|><|start|>assistant to=user<|message|>```python\nimport cadquery as cq\n\n# Outer funnel frustum\nouter = cq.Solid.makeCone(\n    15.2, 28.4, 42.4,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 0),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 1)\n)\n\n# Cylindrical spout outside\nspout = cq.Solid.makeCylinder(\n    8.0, 22.6,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, -18.6),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 1)\n)\n\n# Top rim ring\nrim_outer = cq.Solid.makeCylinder(\n    32.0, 4.1,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 42.4),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 1)\n)\nrim_inner = cq.Solid.makeCylinder(\n    24.9, 4.1,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 42.4),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 1)\n)\nrim = rim_outer.cut(rim_inner)\n\n# Fuse positive parts\npositive = outer.fuse(spout).fuse(rim)\n\n# Inner cone recess\ninner_cone = cq.Solid.makeCone(\n    24.9, 9.9, 35.6,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 43.4),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, -1)\n)\n\n# Bore through spout into recess\nbore = cq.Solid.makeCylinder(\n    4.1, 28.4,\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, -19.6),\n    cq.Vector(0, 0, 1)\n)\n\nresult = positive.cut(inner_cone).cut(bore)\n```<|eot|>"}, "first_loss_tokens": [" to", "=self", "<|message|>", "Units", ":", " mill", "imetres", ".", " Z", " is", " up", ".", " A", " funnel", " on", " the", " Z", " axis", ",", " centred", " at", " X", " =", " ", "0", ",", " Y", " =", " ", "0", ".", " Outer", " cone", " frust", "um", ":", " bottom", " diameter", " ", "30"], "first_loss_tokens_per_source": {"glimmer-first": {"loss_tokens"